Parent naltrexone disposition half-lives: distribution 1.23 h, terminal 9.80 h; smallest Hessian eigenvalue 0.215 (largest 156)
Fixed parameters: fp fixed at 0.95 at the reference dose of 100 mg (the Verebey 1976 dose): the approved naltrexone labelling gives oral bioavailability of 5 to 40 per cent, i.e. a systemic parent fraction (1 - fp) of roughly 0.05 to 0.40; 0.95 sits at the UPPER EDGE of that range (1 - fp = 0.05) and inside the region where the weighted SSE was flat in the R1 free fit (change <= 0.29 for fp 0.9 to 0.999). A dose-dependent structure is anchored at 100 mg, so this value holds there exactly for every D50. Occupancy sensitivity at fp = 0.90 and 0.99 (at 100 mg) is reported in occupancy_sensitivity_J.csv (fits pk_fit_sens_fp090.rds, pk_fit_sens_fp099.rds).
Fixed parameters: fp_D50 fixed at 30 mg (assumed; anchored at 100 mg, so it changes only the shape below 100 mg; D50 = 10 and 100 mg refitted as sensitivities: pk_fit_sens_D50_10.rds, pk_fit_sens_D50_100.rds).
Limitation (stated; no structure added): the chronic (once-daily, steady-state) 6-beta-naltrexol Cmax is predicted 108.7 vs observed 158.4 ng/mL (0.69-fold). With the 24 h concentrations in the fit, linear kinetics cannot also produce the observed 1.8-fold rise of the chronic over the acute peak; Verebey 1976 itself reports no trough accumulation. The observation's CV (0.57) keeps its weight low.
